<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/docs/Doc.abide'
import Example from '#src/docs/Example.abide'
import Section from '#src/docs/Section.abide'
import Snippet from '#src/docs/Snippet.abide'
import Contact from './Contact.abide'
import Counts from './Counts.abide'
import Deal from './Deal.abide'
import Draft from './Draft.abide'
import Profile from './Profile.abide'
import Search from './Search.abide'
import contactSource from './Contact.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import countsSource from './Counts.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import dealSource from './Deal.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import draftSource from './Draft.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import profileSource from './Profile.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import searchSource from './Search.abide' with { type: 'text' }
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="The two body forms of `reactive`">
        <p>A body comes in two forms, and its parameter picks between them.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>You write</th><th>You get</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>reactive(() =&gt; …)</code></td><td>a <code>Reactive</code> — <strong>tracked</strong>, recomputing when what it read changes</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>reactive((args) =&gt; …)</code></td><td>a <code>Keyed</code> — a factory, <strong>untracked</strong>, one entry per args key</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>Both take <code>schema</code>, <code>transform</code>, <code>store</code>,
        <code>global</code>, <code>tags</code>, <code>throttle</code> and <code>debounce</code>, and
        both hand back something a page reads by name. Every signature and every member is in the
        <a href={url('/reference/reactive')}><code>reactive</code> reference</a>.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A derived value reruns when what its body read changes">
        <Example
            id="memo-tracked"
            title="`reactive(() => …)` — an auto-tracked derivation"
            summary="A deal, and the total that follows its seats. Step the seats."
            files={{
                'Deal.abide': dealSource,
            }}
        >
            <Deal />
        </Example>

        <p><em>1 line, 0 dependency lists</em> — against the two variables the hand-written arm
        keeps to recompute no more often than this does.</p>

        <p>Whatever the body <strong>read</strong> on its last run is the whole dependency graph.
        Reading subscribes, so the graph is discovered rather than declared, and it can change from
        one run to the next without anything being kept in step.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A derived value is a `Reactive`, not a wrapper">
        <p>A derived value has no members of its own, so every spelling on it is the one a value you
        write gets:</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>You write</th><th>On a value you write</th><th>On a derived value</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>a read</td><td><code>{'{total}'}</code></td><td><code>{'{total}'}</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td>a probe</td><td><code>contact.busy()</code></td><td><code>contact.busy()</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td>a write</td><td><code>total = 0</code></td><td><code>total = 0</code></td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>There is one <code>set</code> in the design and it is <code>Reactive</code>'s, so writing
        to a derived value is the member the type already declares.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Make a call rerun when its arguments change">
        <Example
            id="memo-adoption"
            title="`reactive(() => call(args))` — a load the value adopts"
            summary="A profile panel behind two tabs. Open the other person, then come back to the first."
            files={{
                'Profile.abide': profileSource,
            }}
        >
            <Profile />
        </Example>

        <p>A <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> setup body is untracked, so the call written bare registers
        nothing and never follows <code>id</code>. Wrapping it in a body is the only spelling that
        does, and that wrapper is compulsory rather than a matter of taste.</p>

        <p>It costs nothing, and that is <strong>adoption</strong>. A body returning another
        <code>Reactive</code> is adopted rather than stored: the outer subscribes to the inner and
        mirrors its productions as its own. Every probe forwards from the adopted value,
        <code>error()</code> included (11.50), and a trigger on the outer reaches the adopted one
        (11.51). So the reads reach the record rather than a box to be opened first, and
        <code>busy</code> belongs to the load. A wrapper that stored the call would settle the
        moment its body returned, and its <code>busy</code> would read false for the whole of the
        load it wraps.</p>

        <p><code>transform</code> runs after that adoption rather than instead of it, on the settled
        payload (11.14). A write does not stop at the outer value, though: it reaches the one
        adopted (11.59), so a reshape you mean to patch reads the load rather than adopting it — see
        <a href={url('/values/patch-a-list-from-a-live-feed')}>Patching a list</a>.</p>

        <p>Adoption is an identity cutoff too: an args key that hits a live entry adopts
        synchronously. Reopening a tab already held is the body running again over a request that
        never happens, and the record's own count says so. <code>opened</code> is kept by the
        handler, which runs once per request that reaches it, so it counts loads. A count taken in
        the browser would count runs of the body, and those do go up.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/data/read-data-without-writing-an-api')}>Reading data</a> ·
        <a href={url('/values/load-once-per-set-of-arguments')}>Caching</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A write to a derived value stands until the next recompute">
        <Example
            id="memo-write"
            title="A write to a derived value stands until the next recompute"
            summary="An edit box seeded from a person the server sent. Type into it, then reload."
            files={{
                'Draft.abide': draftSource,
            }}
        >
            <Draft />
        </Example>

        <p>A derived value is writable, and a write stands until the next recompute clobbers it.
        That means it survives until something the body read moves — one primitive rather than a
        second value beside it and an effect keeping the two in step.</p>

        <p>That is also the trade. The reload is a new answer even where the fields are identical,
        because the cutoff compares identity, and the handler builds a record per call.</p>

        <p>That is a body computing its value. One handing back a <code>Reactive</code> passes the
        write through to the value it adopted instead (11.59).</p>

        <p>There is no warning on that write, and there could not be a useful one: <code>reactive(()
        =&gt; structuredClone(upstream))</code> and <code>reactive(() =&gt; a + b)</code> are
        indistinguishable to a compiler, so a warning would fire hardest on the good pattern.</p>

        <p>A write of a settled value is not a load — <code>ready()</code> stays true and
        <code>busy()</code> never moves. A write of an unsettled one <strong>is</strong> a load and
        moves the probes the way any load does.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A keyed value holds one entry per set of arguments">
        <Example
            id="memo-keyed"
            title="`reactive((args) => …)` — a factory, one entry per key"
            summary="The contact search, filtering as you type. Type toward Ada, then backspace over what you typed."
            files={{
                'Search.abide': searchSource,
            }}
        >
            <Search />
        </Example>

        <p>Every keystroke is a key, so backspacing lands on an entry already held: nothing is asked
        for and <code>busy</code> never gets a moment to be true. The factory does not cap the load.
        Every word not typed before is a request of its own, and <code>throttle</code> caps them. A
        <code>Keyed</code> is <code>(args) =&gt; Reactive</code> with <code>busy</code>,
        <code>ready</code>, <code>invalidate</code> and <code>refresh</code> on it as well — the
        factory holds every key, and so it is the only thing with an args space to narrow.
        <code>{'searchContacts({ query }).busy()'}</code> asks about one entry, and
        <code>searchContacts.busy()</code> about every entry or those a partial args matches
        (13.2).</p>

        <p>An entry does not recompute when something its body read moves (11.6, 14.4).</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/values/load-once-per-set-of-arguments')}>Caching</a> ·
        <a href={url('/values/slow-down-a-value-that-changes-too-fast')}>Throttle &amp; debounce</a>
        · <a href={url('/values/decide-when-a-value-reloads')}>Reloading</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A value read after an `await` stops following">
        <Example
            id="memo-await"
            title="A read after an `await` registers nothing"
            summary="Two counts under one contact filter, differing in one line. Change the filter."
            files={{
                'Counts.abide': countsSource,
            }}
        >
            <Counts />
        </Example>

        <p>Tracking is synchronous. A body suspends at its first <code>await</code> and the
        subscriber is restored, so a reactive read in the continuation subscribes to nothing —
        reliably nothing. The compiler <strong>warns</strong> and names the read, rather than
        leaving you a label that is correct once and stale forever.</p>

        <p>Two repairs, and hoisting the read is also the faster shape: move it above the
        <code>await</code>, or say <code>peek()</code> where the read was meant to be untracked.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/values/decide-when-a-value-reloads')}>Reloading</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A branch on a value that has not landed paints nothing yet">
        <Example
            id="memo-provisional"
            title="`reactive(() => gate ? … : …)` — a branch on a gate that has not landed"
            summary="A contact an auditor may read and not edit. Watch the top Save button, and reload the page to see it again."
            files={{
                'Contact.abide': contactSource,
            }}
        >
            <Contact />
        </Example>

        <p>The body never takes the wrong arm. Reading <code>viewer</code> before it lands throws
        <code>NotLanded</code>, which abandons the run (2.12), and the body runs again when the gate
        lands (2.13). The abandoned run is <strong>provisional</strong> and mints no production
        (11.52): nothing is stored, no <code>transform</code> sees it, no <code>store</code> is
        written, and no reader is served. You write no <code>?.</code>, because nothing past the
        read runs.</p>

        <p>The hole is where the wait shows. <code>mode === 'locked'</code> throws the same way, and
        an abandoned expression leaves what it last rendered in place (32.23). On the first frame
        that is no <code>disabled</code> at all, so the top <code>Save</code> is live until the
        answer arrives.</p>

        <p>Only you know which answer is safe before there is one, so say it with the probe:</p>

        <Snippet source={contactSource} path="Contact.abide" from={['<button disabled={!mode']} />

        <p>Locked is the answer until the gate says otherwise, so the second <code>Save</code> is
        dead on the first frame rather than live for one.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/values/show-a-value-that-isnt-there-yet')}>Loading states</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A read in a condition makes the body re-subscribe">
        <p>A body is tracked by the ORDER of the reads it makes, which is what keeps a recompute to
        a pointer compare per source. <code>flag() ? one() : two()</code> reads a different second
        source every time the flag moves, so the match fails at the flag, everything after it is
        detached, and both sources are subscribed again — per recompute, for as long as the value
        lives.</p>

        <p>The compiler <strong>warns</strong> and names the read (14.15). Two repairs, and both are
        the same move: read the values above the branch so the order is fixed, or give each arm its
        own value and branch over the two. A <code>{'{#if}'}</code> in markup is untouched — a block
        binds each read in its subject once for the body, so the order there cannot alternate.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/values/do-something-when-a-value-changes')}>Watching
        values</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/values/load-once-per-set-of-arguments')}>Caching</a> — one entry per
            set of arguments</li>
            <li><a href={url('/values/slow-down-a-value-that-changes-too-fast')}>Throttle &amp;
            debounce</a> — when the inputs move too fast</li>
            <li><a href={url('/values/do-something-when-a-value-changes')}>Watching values</a> — for
            effects, where a body is for values</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
